mtk: mt_wifi: translate MLME message ids to band steering frame types

BndStrg_CheckConnectionReq() takes the frame type from Elem->MsgType,
which holds the id of the state machine that queued the frame:
SYNC_FSM_PEER_PROBE_REQ (4) for probes and AUTH_FSM_PEER_AUTH_REQ (6)
for auth. The band steering protocol and the RSSI code expect
APMT2_PEER_PROBE_REQ (0) and APMT2_PEER_AUTH_REQ (3), so auth events
reached the daemon with frame type 6 and no RSSI, and the daemon dropped
them.

Translate both ids. This replaces the old 042 patch, which redefined
APMT2_PEER_PROBE_REQ as 4 and so only fixed probes.

Signed-off-by: SuperKali <hello@superkali.me>

--- a/mt_wifi/embedded/ap/ap_band_steering.c
+++ b/mt_wifi/embedded/ap/ap_band_steering.c
@@ -877,7 +877,7 @@ BOOLEAN BndStrg_CheckConnectionReq(
 	MLME_QUEUE_ELEM * Elem,
 	PEER_PROBE_REQ_PARAM * ProbeReqParam)
 {
-	UINT8 FrameType = Elem->MsgType;
+	UINT8 FrameType;
 	CHAR Rssi[4] = {0};
 	PBND_STRG_CLI_TABLE table = NULL;
 	BNDSTRG_MSG msg = { 0 };
@@ -890,6 +890,23 @@ BOOLEAN BndStrg_CheckConnectionReq(
 		return FALSE;
 	}
 
+	/* The MLME state machines pass their own message ids here
+	 * (SYNC_FSM_PEER_PROBE_REQ, AUTH_FSM_PEER_AUTH_REQ), not the APMT2_*
+	 * frame types of the band steering protocol. Translate them, or every
+	 * probe and auth event reaches the daemon with a frame type it does not
+	 * know and without the RSSI that is only filled in for known types. */
+	switch (Elem->MsgType) {
+	case SYNC_FSM_PEER_PROBE_REQ:
+		FrameType = APMT2_PEER_PROBE_REQ;
+		break;
+	case AUTH_FSM_PEER_AUTH_REQ:
+		FrameType = APMT2_PEER_AUTH_REQ;
+		break;
+	default:
+		FrameType = Elem->MsgType;
+		break;
+	}
+
 	table = Get_BndStrgTable(pAd, wdev->func_idx);
 
 	if (!pAd->ApCfg.BndStrgBssIdx[wdev->func_idx])
